# Patch: Kimchi runtime and UI integrations for pi-coding-agent 0.85.1
#
# Tracking: No matching upstream issue exists yet; report remaining generic
# changes to https://github.com/earendil-works/pi before the next upgrade.
# Changes:
#   - Kimchi CLI/provider defaults, extension-triggered prompt handling, forced
#     compaction, model selection, extension UI helpers, and compact visuals.
#   - Extension setModel accepts { persist } and threads it to AgentSession's
#     ModelMutationOptions, restoring the persistence channel upstream made
#     opt-in. setThinkingLevel is deliberately NOT threaded: Kimchi's only
#     caller (src/extensions/tags.ts, set_phase) is tool-driven and must stay
#     session-only, so the plumbing would have no caller. (Upstream candidate.)
#   - exportToJsonl is awaited: src/cli.ts replaces the prototype with an async
#     wrapper (trace IDs + awaited PII redaction, fail-closed). Upstream's method
#     is sync, so this hunk looks redundant - it is not. Dropping it prints
#     [object Promise] and returns before redaction completes.
#   - tools/bash.js timeout pipe-cleanup lives in createLocalShellOperations, so
#     it covers the PowerShell tool too (intentional).
#   Two fixes for third-party npm extensions (e.g. plannotator):
#   - Type2 ReferenceError: Bun's bundler renames Google GenAI's `Type` enum to
#     `Type2` (collision with typebox's `Type`); the pi-ai compat module re-exports
#     `Type` from typebox, but the bundled getter references the broken `Type2`
#     binding. A Proxy overrides `.Type` with the typebox namespace directly.
#     Only observable in the compiled binary.
#   - Flag conflict crash: when an npm extension registers a flag that kimchi
#     already provides (e.g. `--plan`), the conflict is downgraded from a hard
#     error to a warning (kimchi built-in extensions load first and win).
# Security (project trust): extends hasTrustRequiringProjectResources
# (dist/core/trust-manager.js) with Kimchi's project-local .kimchi/ and .claude/
# resources (config, permissions, hooks, skills, agents, memory, tags, plans,
# ferments, MCP servers, pi-agent skills) so a cloned repo that ships them triggers the trust
# prompt instead of being auto-trusted. The list is checked in cwd AND its
# ancestors (matching the ancestor-walking skills/tags readers), stopping at
# the user's home (a user resource) and the OS temp directory (not a project
# tree — a stray .kimchi/.claude created there by any tool must not gate
# unrelated temp-dir sessions). The trust prompt and the untrusted warning
# (dist/core/project-trust.js, dist/modes/interactive/interactive-mode.js)
# mention those resources and speak as Kimchi, not pi.
# Careful: pasteToEditor is NOT patched here - src/paste-to-editor-patch.ts owns
#   that routing. Do not re-add a dist hunk for it.
# Removal: delete each hunk once the pinned Pi release exposes equivalent APIs
# or Kimchi no longer needs the branded behavior. For the trust hunks: remove
# once Pi ships native trust-scan extensibility or Kimchi adopts the
# Pi-standard configDir layout. NOTE: the entry list must stay in sync with
# TRUST_REQUIRING_PROJECT_RESOURCES in src/project-scope-trust.ts — a unit
# test cross-checks the two.
# History: see patches/CHANGELOG.md.
#
